Drug-Induced Acute Pancreatitis secondary to Sodium Valproate therapy

## 1. Case Scenario and Patient Narrative

### Initial Information
* Report Type: Initial Spontaneous Report
* Country of Origin: India
* Primary Source: Healthcare Professional (Gastroenterologist / Physician)

### Patient Demographics
* Patient Initials: P.V.
* Age: 26 years
* Gender: Female
* Weight: 58 kg
* Relevant Medical History: Generalized Tonic-Clonic Seizures (6 months). No prior history of gallbladder disease, alcohol consumption, or hypertriglyceridemia.

### Suspected and Concomitant Drugs
* Suspected Drug: Sodium Valproate 500 mg orally twice daily (Indication: Generalized Epilepsy; Duration: Initiated 4 weeks prior to emergency admission).
* Interacting Drug: None.
* Concomitant Drug: None.

### Case Narrative and Timeline
* Week 1: Patient initiated on Sodium Valproate 500 mg twice daily.
* Week 4 (Day 1): Patient presented to an emergency care department in India with severe, constant epigastric pain radiating directly to the back, accompanied by persistent vomiting and low-grade fever (38.1°C).
* Week 4 (Day 1 Diagnostic Findings):
  * Serum Amylase: 1,180 U/L (Reference: 30 to 110 U/L).
  * Serum Lipase: 1,450 U/L (Reference: 10 to 140 U/L; >10x Upper Limit of Normal).
  * Abdominal Ultrasound & Contrast CT: Diffuse pancreatic enlargement with peripancreatic fluid collection; no gallstones or biliary duct dilatation observed.
  * Serum Triglycerides & Calcium: Normal limits.
* Action Taken: Sodium Valproate was immediately and permanently discontinued. Patient was admitted to the High Dependency Unit (HDU) for IV fluid resuscitation, bowel rest, and pain management. Levetiracetam was initiated for seizure control.
* Outcome: By Day 10 post-discontinuation, abdominal pain resolved, oral intake was tolerated, serum amylase and lipase normalized, and the patient was discharged without long-term pancreatic insufficiency.

